<h1>Chapter 5 — Stewardship</h1>

<p class="lead">
  The maker is a temporary custodian of form, not its absolute owner.
</p>

<h2>I. Work as Continuation</h2>

<p>
  Every object enters a longer stream of use, care, and interpretation. It is shaped not only for its first moment of encounter, but for the life it will live after the maker has finished.
</p>

<p>
  This perspective changes the priorities of making. The object is not merely completed; it is entrusted.
</p>

<h2>II. Responsibility to Material</h2>

<p>
  Stewardship means protecting the integrity of material rather than imposing a transient style upon it. A work that disregards the conditions of its own making will age poorly, both physically and conceptually.
</p>

<p>
  Good stewardship recognizes that a crafted thing must remain legible to future hands and future eyes.
</p>

<h2>III. The Ethics of Care</h2>

<p>
  The mature maker understands that their role is not to dominate a material but to guide it with care and humility. This kind of attention produces objects that are more durable, more coherent, and more generous.
</p>

<p>
  Stewardship therefore becomes a practical and moral form of judgment.
</p>

